body:
## What it is

The everyday value types every loft program uses: whole numbers (`integer`), decimals (`float`), yes/no (`boolean`), and single characters (`character`). `single` is the 32-bit decimal — half the space and the width a GPU buffer, a mesh vertex or an audio sample wants; `float` is the 64-bit one and is what a decimal literal is.

Each of them spends one of its own values on `null` rather than paying for a separate flag — see @F1 for which value that is per type.

## How it aids you

These are the building blocks you reach for constantly. Each variable says which one it holds, so the meaning of your data is clear and a slip like putting text where a number belongs is caught for you.

## Example

```loft
fn main() {
    count: integer = 42;
    ratio: float = 3.5;
    ok: boolean = true;
    letter: character = 'A';
    println("{count} {ratio} {ok} {letter}");   // 42 3.5 true A
}
```

## In real use

Where this feature carries real work, validated by `check_doc_drift.sh examples`:

Example: @MSH-002 — a mesh flattened for upload is a `vector<single>`, and the stride is what the consumer has to be told

## What it is

An INDEX over a set of records, read by a key field. You give it a value for that field and it hands back the matching record straight away, with no searching through a list. A key can be several fields — that is the `[keys]` in `hash<T[keys]>` — and the lookup then takes one value per key: `hash<Cell[x, y]>` is read `grid.cells[1, 2]`.

It is an index rather than a container, and that shows the moment a struct declares two of them over the SAME element type: they are two routes to ONE record set, not two collections. Append through either and both see it, and `len` moves on both. That is a multi-index table for the cost of one extra field declaration — and it is why filling both members of such a group from one literal earns `advice[linked-group-double-fill]`: the records do not divide between them.

## How it aids you

When you need to find "the record with this name" over and over, a hash gives you the answer directly instead of scanning everything each time. You declare which field is the key, so every lookup reads naturally.

A key is unique, so `+=` with a key already present REPLACES that record rather than adding a second one — the count does not move. A lookup that finds nothing answers `null`, and reading a field off that miss stays `null`; nothing is created by asking.

## Example

```loft
struct Person { name: text, age: integer }
struct Directory { people: hash<Person[name]> }
fn main() {
    dir = Directory { };
    dir.people = [ {name: "Ada", age: 36}, {name: "Bo", age: 20} ];
    dir.people += [ {name: "Cy", age: 51} ];
    println("Ada is {dir.people["Ada"].age}, Cy is {dir.people["Cy"].age}");
    // Ada is 36, Cy is 51
    dir.people += [ {name: "Ada", age: 37} ];   // same key — REPLACES, still 3 records
    println("{dir.people["Ada"].age} {dir.people["Zed"] == null}");   // 37 true
}
```

## In real use

Where this feature carries real work, validated by `check_doc_drift.sh examples`:

Example: @FTR-011 — two keyed fields over one element type are two routes to ONE record set, and `hash<T[keys]>` takes a compound key

## What it is

A collection of records that stays in sorted order by a key field you choose. New records drop into the right position on their own, and looping over the collection visits them in that order. A leading `-` on the key means order it the other way round.

## How it aids you

You never have to re-sort by hand. Add records whenever you like and the collection is always in order — ideal for leaderboards, rankings, or anything you want to read back highest-first or lowest-first.

## Example

```loft
struct Item { name: text, score: integer }
struct Board { items: sorted<Item[-score]> }
fn main() {
    b = Board { items: [
        Item { name: "Ada", score: 30 },
        Item { name: "Bo", score: 90 },
        Item { name: "Cy", score: 60 }
    ] };
    for it in b.items {
        println("{it.name}: {it.score}");
    }
    // Bo: 90
    // Cy: 60
    // Ada: 30
}
```



## In real use

Where this feature carries real work, validated by `check_doc_drift.sh examples`:

Example: @FTR-037 — a `sorted` collection keeps its order as records arrive, descending on a `-key`, and answers a lookup by that key

## What it is

A collection of records kept in order by one or more key fields. You can jump straight to a record by its keys, and you can also walk through a whole ordered range of them. A leading `-` on a key orders it the other way round.

## How it aids you

It combines instant lookup with ordered iteration over more than one field at once — group by a primary key, break ties with a second, and read the records back in order.

## Example

```loft
struct Row { group: integer, name: text }
struct Db { rows: index<Row[group, name]> }
fn main() {
    db = Db { rows: [
        Row { group: 2, name: "b" },
        Row { group: 1, name: "a" },
        Row { group: 1, name: "c" }
    ] };
    for r in db.rows {
        println("{r.group}-{r.name}");
    }
    println("lookup 1,a -> {db.rows[1, "a"].group}");
    // 1-a
    // 1-c
    // 2-b
    // lookup 1,a -> 1
}
```



## In real use

Where this feature carries real work, validated by `check_doc_drift.sh examples`:

Example: @FTR-038 — an `index` on a multi-part key: lookup by key, ordered iteration, and a second key breaking ties in the other direction

## What it is

A lazy sequence of values you can loop over one at a time. It produces each value on demand as the loop asks for it, instead of building a whole list up front. The `for` loops you write over ranges and vectors are powered by iterators.

## How it aids you

You can walk through a sequence — a range of numbers, the items of a collection — without ever building or storing the full list, so a loop over a million values costs no extra memory.

## Example

```loft
fn main() {
    total = 0;
    for i in 1..5 {          // steps through 1, 2, 3, 4 one at a time
        total += i;
    }
    println("total={total}");
    // total=10
}
```



## In real use

Where this feature carries real work, validated by `check_doc_drift.sh examples`:

Example: @FTR-039 — an `iterator<T>` value: `yield`, `yield from`, `next()`, `exhausted()`, and a for-loop over it

## What it is

A fixed-size group of values that can each be a different type, written in parentheses like `(3, "three")`. You read the parts by position with `.0`, `.1`, and so on.

## How it aids you

When you want to pass or return a few related values together — a low and a high, or a code and a message — a tuple does it in one step, with no need to define a named type first.

## Example

```loft
fn min_max(a: integer, b: integer) -> (integer, integer) {
    if a <= b { (a, b) } else { (b, a) }
}
fn main() {
    pair: (integer, text) = (3, "three");
    println("{pair.0} = {pair.1}");
    (lo, hi) = min_max(9, 4);
    println("lo={lo} hi={hi}");
    // 3 = three
    // lo=4 hi=9
}
```

## Writing an element, and a reference tuple

An element is assignable like an ordinary variable, and a `&` parameter lets a function write the caller's tuple in place:

```loft
fn swap(p: &(integer, integer)) {
    t = p.0;
    p.0 = p.1;
    p.1 = t;
}

fn main() {
    a = (1, 2);
    swap(a);                       // note: no `&` at the call — the parameter type carries it
    println("{a.0}{a.1}");         // 21
}
```

## Where a `text` element still catches

A tuple holding text reads, interpolates, returns and lives in a vector on both backends, and writing one lands where it is aimed. Two shapes do not work yet, both loudly:

- passing a tuple **variable** with a text element to a function is refused by `--native` at compile time (loft#1005) — the same tuple written as a **literal** argument is fine, as is splitting the elements into separate parameters;
- a `&(text, …)` reference-tuple parameter is an internal compiler error (loft#1006). Use a struct where the elements are text: a struct parameter already names the caller's record, so it writes through with no `&` at all.

Until loft#1004 the write itself was the problem, and silently: `a.0 = "X"` on a `(text, text)` changed `.1`, a write to the last element was discarded, and a write next to a non-text element segfaulted the interpreter.

## In real use

Where this feature carries real work, validated by `check_doc_drift.sh examples`:

Example: @FTR-031 — every tuple spelling: literal, element read and write, by-value and reference parameters, a tuple return, and LHS destructuring
Example: @FTR-030 — a tuple's text element is written where it is aimed, at every index and width

## What it is

A type that is exactly one of a fixed set of named options.

## How it aids you

When something can only be one of a few choices, an enum names them all in one place — you can't use an invalid value, and a `match` can make sure you have handled every case.

## Example

```loft
enum Direction { North, East, South, West }
fn main() {
    d: Direction = East;
    label = match d { North | South => "vertical", East | West => "horizontal" };
    println(label);    // horizontal
}
```


## In real use

Where this feature carries real work, validated by `check_doc_drift.sh examples`:

Example: @FTR-032 — a plain enum's ordering, text conversion and vector storage, and a struct enum's construction, field access and polymorphic dispatch

## What it is

An enum whose choices each carry their own fields. One `Shape` type can be a `Circle` with a radius or a `Rect` with a width and height — every variant keeps exactly the data it needs.

## How it aids you

You keep a family of related shapes under one type, and you can give each variant its own version of a method. Calling `area()` runs the right one automatically, so your code stays simple no matter which variant it is holding.

## Example

```loft
enum Shape {
    Circle { radius: float },
    Rect { width: float, height: float }
}
fn area(self: Circle) -> float { 3.14 * self.radius * self.radius }
fn area(self: Rect) -> float { self.width * self.height }
fn main() {
    c = Circle { radius: 2.0 };
    r = Rect { width: 3.0, height: 4.0 };
    println("circle={c.area()} rect={r.area()}");
    // circle=12.56 rect=12
}
```



## In real use

Where this feature carries real work, validated by `check_doc_drift.sh examples`:

Example: @FTR-032 — a struct enum's per-variant fields, construction, field access and polymorphic dispatch

## What it is

When the type is already known — a variable's declared type, a function parameter, a return value — you can write a variant name on its own, like `East`, without repeating the enum name. Loft works out which enum it belongs to from the surrounding context.

## How it aids you

Your code reads cleaner: no need to spell out `Direction.East` every time when it is already clear a `Direction` is expected. It is shorter to write and easier to read, and the compiler still checks the name is a real variant of that enum.

## Example

```loft
enum Direction { North, East, South, West }
fn turn_around(d: Direction) -> Direction {
    match d { North => South, South => North, East => West, West => East }
}
fn main() {
    d: Direction = East;
    println("{turn_around(d)}");
    // West
}
```



## In real use

Where this feature carries real work, validated by `check_doc_drift.sh examples`:

Example: @FTR-032 — a bare variant name where the enum is known from context, beside the qualified `Direction.East` spelling

## What it is

Put `const` in front of a parameter's type to say this function does not change that value. Every write THROUGH the parameter is a compile error — `p.f = …`, `p[i] = …`, `p += …`, and nested writes under them — so the read-only accessor style is checked rather than promised in a comment.

What it does not yet stop is the value being handed ON. A `const` parameter can still be passed to a function whose own parameter is not `const`, and that callee's write reaches the original record — a plain struct parameter names the caller's value (@F106), so nothing about the second call site says the first one promised anything. Closing that is @PLN40 steps 4–5 in the loft repo; until then, read `const` as *this function does not write it*, not as *nobody downstream writes it*.

## How it aids you

Anyone reading the signature knows this function will not write your data, and if its body ever tries, the compiler says so straight away instead of leaving you to chase a surprise. That is what makes an accessor-style API — `length3(v: const Vec3)`, `mesh_to_floats(m: const Mesh)` — worth reading: the promise is in the type, checked, and costs no copy.

Where the value travels further, follow it: a `const` value handed to a non-`const` parameter is not refused today.

## Example

```loft
struct Account {
    balance: integer
}
fn describe(acct: const Account) -> text {
    "balance is {acct.balance}"
}
fn main() {
    a = Account { balance: 500 };
    println(describe(a));           // balance is 500
    println("still {a.balance}");   // still 500
}
```

## In real use

Where this feature carries real work, validated by `check_doc_drift.sh examples`:

Example: @MSH-002 — `mesh3d` declares its whole read-only surface `const` (`mesh_to_floats(mf: const Mesh)`, `add3(va: const Vec3, …)`), so flattening a mesh for upload cannot disturb the mesh

## What it is

When a value can be one of several shapes — a Shape is either a Circle or a Rect — you write one version of a method for each shape, all with the same name. Loft runs the right one automatically, based on which shape the value actually holds.

## How it aids you

You call `shape.area()` and get the correct answer without checking which kind of shape it is yourself. Add a new shape later and you simply add its version of the method — the code that does the calling stays exactly the same.

## Example

```loft
enum Shape {
    Circle { radius: float },
    Rect { width: float, height: float }
}
fn area(self: Circle) -> float {
    3.14159 * self.radius * self.radius
}
fn area(self: Rect) -> float {
    self.width * self.height
}
fn main() {
    c = Circle { radius: 2.0 };
    r = Rect { width: 3.0, height: 4.0 };
    println("circle area = {c.area()}");   // circle area = 12.56636
    println("rect area = {r.area()}");     // rect area = 12
}
```



## In real use

Where this feature carries real work, validated by `check_doc_drift.sh examples`:

Example: @FTR-032 — one method name written per variant, dispatched on the variant the value holds

## What it is

Put `&` before a parameter's type to pass a variable by reference. The function then works on your actual variable, so any change it makes stays after the call returns.

`&` is what a **scalar** needs. Without it an `integer`, `float`, `boolean`, `character` or `text` parameter is a copy: the callee's write is gone when the call returns, and the new value has to travel home in the return instead.

A **struct, value struct or collection** parameter is not a copy — it names the caller's own record, so a field or element write through it lands with or without `&`. loft says so itself: a `&` whose only write is a field earns `advice[slow-reference-parameter]`, *"field mutation already propagates to the caller without it"*.

What `&` still buys for those types is **replacing the whole binding**. `s = S { … }` inside the callee reaches the caller only through `&`; without it the new value dies with the call, and nothing at the call site says so.

## How it aids you

Let a function update your data in place — add to a running total, adjust a counter, hand back a parsed value through an out-parameter — without you having to catch a result and assign it back. For a scalar that is the difference between a helper that can update your counter and one that cannot; for a struct or a collection it is the visible way to say the callee may replace the whole thing, not just write into it.

## Example

```loft
fn add_to(x: &integer, amount: integer) {
    x += amount;
}
fn main() {
    total = 10;
    add_to(total, 5);
    add_to(total, 100);
    println("total = {total}");    // total = 115
}
```


## One rule to know

A `&` is an ownership decision, so loft keeps its promise or refuses the program — it will
never quietly hand you a copy instead. Taking a reference into a container and then
**disturbing that container while the reference is still in use** is a compile-time error:
removing from it, writing a key field through the reference, or replacing the container
outright all end the place the reference names.

```loft
c = &v[0];
c.n = 99;      // fine — finished with `c` before `v` changes
v.remove(2);

c = &v[0];
v.remove(2);   // error: cannot remove from `v` while `c` references a place inside it
c.n = 99;
```

Only *liveness* matters, so moving the last use of the reference above the change is the
usual fix. A plain (non-`&`) binding is unaffected: it gets its own copy and is told so.

## In real use

Where this feature carries real work, validated by `check_doc_drift.sh examples`:

Example: @FTR-003 — which argument kinds a callee's write reaches, and what `&` still buys

## What it is

A closure is a small anonymous function you can keep in a variable, hand to another function, or return. It remembers the values it uses from around it, taken at the moment you create it.

## How it aids you

Write a short piece of behaviour right where you need it — a callback, a quick rule, or a function that builds another function — without naming a separate function for each.

A **scalar** capture is a snapshot taken when the closure is made, so reassigning the variable afterwards does not reach it — the example below shows exactly that. A **struct or collection** capture is not a snapshot: it travels as a reference to the same record, so the closure and the enclosing scope hold one value and writes cross in both directions for as long as both are alive.

That sharing is what makes a closure usable as a handler rather than only as a rule: a server's event closure appends to the world it captured, and the appends are still there on the next event.

## Example

```loft
fn make_adder(n: integer) -> fn(integer) -> integer {
    fn(x: integer) -> integer { n + x }
}
fn main() {
    add5 = make_adder(5);
    println("add5(10) = {add5(10)}");      // add5(10) = 15
    greeting = "Hello";
    greet = fn(name: text) -> text { "{greeting}, {name}" };
    greeting = "Bye";
    println(greet("world"));               // Hello, world
}
```

## In real use

Where this feature carries real work, validated by `check_doc_drift.sh examples`:

Example: @EHK-001 — a server kernel's event handler is a closure over the world it mutates
Example: @FTR-004 — a scalar capture is a snapshot; a struct or collection capture is shared both ways

## What it is

Three ready-made tools for working with a whole vector at once. `map` makes a new vector by transforming every element, `filter` keeps only the elements that pass a test, and `reduce` combines all the elements into a single value.

## How it aids you

Say what you want done to the list — double each one, keep the even ones, add them all up — instead of writing the loop and the bookkeeping by hand. It is shorter to write, harder to get wrong, and each line reads as one clear step.

## Example

```loft
fn main() {
    nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
    doubled = map(nums, |x| { x * 2 });
    evens = filter(nums, |x| { x % 2 == 0 });
    total = reduce(nums, 0, |acc, x| { acc + x });
    println("{doubled}");          // [2,4,6,8,10]
    println("{evens}");            // [2,4]
    println("total = {total}");    // total = 15
}
```

## Three more, for a yes/no question

`any` and `all` answer a boolean and stop as soon as they know — `any` at the first element that passes, `all` at the first that fails — and `count_if` counts the ones that pass. They take the same `(vector, predicate)` shape as `filter`, and a predicate must take exactly one argument and answer a boolean.

```loft
fn main() {
    nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
    limit = 3;
    println("{any(nums, |x| { x > limit })}");        // true
    println("{all(nums, |x| { x > limit })}");        // false
    println("{count_if(nums, |x| { x > limit })}");   // 2
}
```

All six take a named function, a bare lambda, a lambda that CAPTURES (`limit` above), or a fn-ref held in a variable. The three predicate calls used to accept only the first two spellings and discard the call outright for the other two — no loop, no diagnostic, the destination keeping its previous value (loft#1001, fixed).

## The vector is new; the value your callback gets is not

"Makes a new vector" is a promise about the vector, and it holds: the result is its own vector with its own records, so writing into what `map` or `filter` answered never reaches back into the input.

It is not a promise about the value your callback is handed. A `struct` element is a record, and a parameter names that record — the caller's — so a callback that writes to its parameter edits the SOURCE vector:

```loft
struct Row { name: text, n: integer }

fn main() {
    rows = [Row { name: "a", n: 1 }, Row { name: "b", n: 2 }];
    bumped = map(rows, |r| { r.n = 99; r });
    println("{rows[0].n},{rows[1].n}");        // 99,99 — the input moved
    println("{bumped[0].n},{bumped[1].n}");    // 99,99
}
```

A scalar or a text element is copied, so the identical shape leaves the input alone — `map(scal, |x| { x = x * 10; x })` answers `[10,20,30]` and `scal` is still `[1,2,3]`. Which it is depends on the element TYPE, not on how the callback is written, and it is the same in `map`, `filter`, `reduce`, `any`, a comprehension and a plain `for` loop. Build a fresh value in the callback (`Row { name: r.name, n: 99 }`) when the input must not move.

## "Every element" has exceptions today

The loop these six build ends when the element it just fetched reads as *nothing*, and for five element types a real element reads that way — or never does. An `integer`, `float`, `character`, `text` or `struct` element is fine; a `boolean`, a nullable holding `null`, a `vector`, an `enum` or a `value struct` is not (loft#1000):

```loft
fn main() {
    flags = [true, false, true];
    println("{len(map(flags, |b| { b }))}");   // three elements in, answers 1
}
```

An `enum` or `value struct` element does not terminate at all, and a `vector` element answers an empty vector on the interpreter while `--native` refuses to build the program. Until that is fixed, write those five as a `for` statement and build the result by hand — a `for` statement counts its elements and is correct for every element type:

```loft
fn main() {
    flags = [true, false, true];
    out: vector<integer> = [];
    for b in flags { out += [if b { 1 } else { 0 }]; }
    println("{out}");                          // [1,0,1]
}
```

## In real use

Where this feature carries real work, validated by `check_doc_drift.sh examples`:

Example: @FTR-026 — map / filter / reduce over a vector, and which half of "a new vector" is new
Example: @FTR-027 — the other three of the family: a predicate over a whole vector, in every spelling a predicate has

## What it is

A generic function uses a stand-in type, written `<T>`, so one function works for any type. Write "give me the first element of a vector" once, and it works for numbers, for words, or for anything else you pass in.

## How it aids you

Write a piece of logic a single time instead of keeping one near-identical copy per type. Loft fills in the real type for each call, so you get the convenience of one function together with the safety of exact type checking.

## Example

```loft
fn first<T>(v: vector<T>) -> T {
    v[0]
}
fn main() {
    ints = [10, 20, 30];
    words = ["alpha", "beta", "gamma"];
    println("{first(ints)}");     // 10
    println("{first(words)}");    // alpha
}
```



## In real use

Where this feature carries real work, validated by `check_doc_drift.sh examples`:

Example: @FTR-041 — one generic body instantiated at four different types, inferred from the call site

## What it is

A bound like `<T: Ordered>` lets a generic function require that its type can do certain things — here, be compared with `<` and `>`. An interface names a group of abilities (Ordered for comparing, Addable for adding, and so on) that a type either has or does not.

## How it aids you

Write one function that works for every type that can be compared — numbers, text, and more — while Loft guarantees you only use abilities each type genuinely has. You get broad reuse without giving up the checks that catch mistakes early.

## Example

```loft
fn largest<T: Ordered>(a: T, b: T) -> T {
    if a > b { a } else { b }
}
fn main() {
    println("{largest(3, 7)}");        // 7
    println("{largest(2.5, 1.5)}");    // 2.5
    best = largest("apple", "pear");
    println(best);                     // pear
}
```

## In real use

Where this feature carries real work, validated by `check_doc_drift.sh examples`:

Example: @STD-005 — `sum` over any Addable type, with the identity supplied by the caller
Example: @STD-006 — one breadth-first tree walk written against the `Walkable` interface

## What it is

`is` tests whether an enum value is a particular variant — and, in the same step, can pull that variant's own fields out into named values you use right there.

## How it aids you

You check what a value is and reach its data in a single line, with no separate lookup or cast. Whatever you name in the braces is in scope inside the block, and only there.

## Example

```loft
enum Shape {
    Circle { radius: integer },
    Square { side: integer }
}

fn describe(s: Shape) -> text {
    if s is Circle { radius } {
        return "circle of radius {radius}";
    }
    if s is Square { side } {
        return "square of side {side}";
    }
    "unknown"
}

fn main() {
    println(describe(Circle { radius: 5 }));  // circle of radius 5
    println(describe(Square { side: 3 }));    // square of side 3
}
```


## The braces after the variant are always the capture list

That rule is what makes the capture unambiguous, and it has one consequence worth knowing before you write it: the expression form does not parse.

```loft
v = if c is Circle { radius } else { 0 };     // error: Expect token {
```

`{ radius }` is read as the capture list, so the parser then wants a body block and finds `else`. The message does not say so (loft#1007). Two spellings do work — write the name twice, or use `match`, which takes captures and IS an expression:

```loft
v = if c is Circle { radius } { radius } else { 0 };            // 5
v = match c { Circle { radius } => radius, Square { side } => side };   // 5
```

A captured name shadows an outer one of the same spelling for the block and hands it back afterwards, and using it after the block is a compile error rather than a stale read — "only there" is enforced, not merely intended.

## In real use

Where this feature carries real work, validated by `check_doc_drift.sh examples`:

Example: @FTR-034 — `is` as a variant test, and the field capture that comes with it: one field, several, the wrong variant, an `else`, inside a loop, and the name not leaking past the block

## What it is

`break` stops a loop early and `continue` skips straight to its next turn. To act on an outer loop from inside a nested one, name that loop's variable: `i#break` breaks the loop over `i`, and `i#continue` skips to its next turn.

## How it aids you

You leave a loop the moment you have what you need instead of running it to the end, and you can jump right out of several nested loops at once — no extra flags or bookkeeping.

## Example

```loft
fn main() {
    // continue: skip the value 3, add the rest
    sum = 0;
    for n in 1..=5 {
        if n == 3 { continue; }
        sum += n;
    }
    println("sum without 3: {sum}");                     // sum without 3: 12

    // break out of the OUTER loop from inside the inner one
    found = "";
    for i in 1..=3 {
        for j in 1..=3 {
            if i * j == 4 {
                found = "{i} and {j}";
                i#break;
            }
        }
    }
    println("first pair whose product is 4: {found}");   // first pair whose product is 4: 2 and 2
}
```

## Which loops can be named

Labelling names a loop's VARIABLE, so it reaches a `for` and not a `while` — a `while`
has no variable to name, and there is no other way to leave an outer `while` from inside
an inner loop. A labelled break does CROSS a `while` on its way out, so an inner `while`
nested in a `for i` can still leave that `for` with `i#break`.

The name must be a real loop variable. An ordinary local currently crashes the compiler
instead of being diagnosed (loft#998).

